GMP 6.3.0's bundled configure script has an ancient K&R-style self-test
("long long reliability test 1", extracted from tests/mpn/t-iord_u.c to work
around an old Apple GCC 3.3 powerpc segfault) that declares `void g(){}` and
then calls it with 6 arguments. Older GCC treated an empty-parens
declaration as an unprototyped/K&R "unspecified arguments" signature and
allowed this; GCC 15's tightened C conformance now hard-rejects it as a real
error ("too many arguments to function 'g'; expected 0, have 6"), which
makes ALL THREE of configure's ABI probes (m64/x32/m32) fail identically and
aborts GCCcore's own "stage2_stuff" bootstrap sub-build of GMP (a separate
PIC-static configure/build step, distinct from - and not covered by - the
existing GCCcore-9.3.0_gmp-c99.patch, which only patches the in-tree
Makefile.in path).

Fix: give `g` a real prototype matching how it's actually called, so it
compiles cleanly under both old and new GCC. `g`'s body is empty either way
(it exists purely so the code compiles/links, not for its behavior), so this
changes nothing about what the test actually checks.

author: this repo (eri-easyconfigs), 2026-08-18

Applied via the easyconfig's `('...patch', '../gmp-6.3.0')` sourcepath
redirect (GMP is a separately-extracted sibling of gcc-15.2.0, not a
subdirectory of it) - so this patch is written relative to gmp-6.3.0's own
top-level directory, not gcc's.
--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -6568,7 +6568,7 @@

 #if defined (__GNUC__) && ! defined (__cplusplus)
 typedef unsigned long long t1;typedef t1*t2;
-void g(){}
+void g(int p0,t2 p1,t1 p2,t2 p3,t2 p4,int p5){}
 void h(){}
 static __inline__ t1 e(t2 rp,t2 up,int n,t1 v0)
 {t1 c,x,r;int i;if(v0){c=1;for(i=1;i<n;i++){x=up[i];r=x+1;rp[i]=r;}}return c;}
@@ -8187,7 +8187,7 @@

 #if defined (__GNUC__) && ! defined (__cplusplus)
 typedef unsigned long long t1;typedef t1*t2;
-void g(){}
+void g(int p0,t2 p1,t1 p2,t2 p3,t2 p4,int p5){}
 void h(){}
 static __inline__ t1 e(t2 rp,t2 up,int n,t1 v0)
 {t1 c,x,r;int i;if(v0){c=1;for(i=1;i<n;i++){x=up[i];r=x+1;rp[i]=r;}}return c;}
